# scripts/provision.env.example — PROVISIONING-TIME contract: the operator's
# local, gitignored .env at the repo root, read by scripts/provision-vps.sh
# and scripts/provision-supabase-stack.sh. Host-side, human-run-once, never
# injected into any container.
#
# ⚠ This is NOT the app/worker RUNTIME contract. That's the repo-root
# .env.example (Coolify-injected on the box, V1 web-app container scope) —
# see its own header for why the split exists and stays explicit: mixing a
# provisioning-time token into a container's env, or vice versa, is exactly
# the confinement violation both files' own conventions exist to prevent.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT merged into the repo-root .env.example. That file's own
# header states its scope precisely: "ONLY the secrets the V1 web-app
# container is permitted to hold" — enumerating only that surface's secrets
# IS the security property there. These variables are a different surface
# entirely, so they get their own example file rather than diluting that
# one's invariant. Both live under a gitignored `.env` at the repo root at
# runtime — the split is about what's DOCUMENTED where, not where the real
# file goes.
#
# Copy the lines you need into the real, gitignored .env at the repo root.
# Never commit a filled-in copy of this file.
#
# ── THE OPERATOR-PROVIDED SET — this file is the COMPLETE contract ─────────
# Re-scoped 2026-09-20 (BACKLOG.md §7.36 item 68, W-2 rider — F/CTO asked
# "is the .env.example template up to date?"; measured answer was NO, this
# file was missing the push-production-secrets.sh-read production_only set
# entirely). This file's OWN claim to completeness is no longer a hand-
# maintained prose count (the "eleven names" framing this replaced went
# stale the moment a new script or a new manifest entry landed without a
# matching edit here) — it is enforced by `scripts/ci/fence-operator-env-
# template.sh`, which derives the live set of names any `scripts/*.sh`
# reads from `$REPO_ROOT/.env` (grepping the `read_env_var`/`env_or_dotenv`
# call-site idiom and the direct `grep -m1 '^NAME=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env"`
# idiom) UNION the `secrets-manifest.yml` `production_only` names
# `push-production-secrets.sh` itself reads from `.env` (its own
# manifest-driven read, minus its own stated `EXCLUDED_SUPABASE_STACK` /
# `EXCLUDED_DEFERRED` sets), and fails CI if any resulting name is absent
# from this file. Grouped below by where the value comes from and what
# reads it, per that same fence's own three source categories — not a
# ceiling, since the fence re-derives the set live on every PR.
#
# ⚠ NOT every plausible-looking name belongs here — verified against the
# tree before adding, not assumed from the name alone. `COOLIFY_API_TOKEN`
# is box-generated (see BOX-GENERATED below); `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` (bare, not
# `PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`) appears only in a comment, never read from any
# local `.env` by any script; grepping this whole tree for `porkbun` (any
# case) returns zero hits — §2's DNS-registrar step is entirely BY-HAND
# (a human sets records in the registrar console), no script reads a
# registrar API credential from `.env` today. None of the three get a line
# below; if any of them is later wired to a real script read, the fence
# will catch its absence the same PR that adds the read.

# --- Hetzner (scripts/provision-vps.sh, Phase 1) ---------------------------
# Where: Hetzner Cloud Console -> the project -> Security -> API Tokens ->
# Generate. Verify that path live — the console moves.
# Permission: Read & Write. The script creates servers, primary IPs,
# firewalls, and SSH keys — a read-only token cannot do any of that.
# Lifecycle: needed only while provision-vps.sh runs. Never enters Coolify or
# this repo. Revoke it in the console after the box is up, if you prefer not
# to leave it live.
# Renamed 2026-09-10 from HETZNER_API_KEY — Hetzner calls it an API Token,
# sent as a bearer; this repo's own convention is _TOKEN for bearer tokens
# (see SIMPLEFIN_TOKEN) and _KEY for actual keys. The script does not accept
# the old name as a fallback.
HETZNER_API_TOKEN=<hetzner-api-token>

# --- Coolify admin (scripts/provision-vps.sh, Phase 2 admin bootstrap) -----
# The ONE human-chosen credential in the whole scripted stand-up — everything
# else provision-vps.sh/provision-supabase-stack.sh touch is generated or
# minted on the box. A generated password is secure and unusable; a human
# needs to be able to log into the Coolify dashboard with something they
# picked. COOLIFY_ADMIN_EMAIL / COOLIFY_ADMIN_NAME are non-secret and may
# stay in .env indefinitely. COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD is a real secret — delete
# the line after the first successful run if you'd rather not leave it
# sitting in a local file. If any of the three is missing and the script is
# run from an interactive terminal, it prompts instead of failing — set
# these only if you want a fully unattended run.
COOLIFY_ADMIN_EMAIL=<your-email>
COOLIFY_ADMIN_NAME=<your-name>
COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<a-password-you-will-actually-remember>

# --- Outbound auth email (scripts/provision-supabase-stack.sh) -------------
# Operator-provided, like HETZNER_API_TOKEN/COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD above —
# NOT box-generated the way POSTGRES_PASSWORD/JWT_SECRET are (see the
# BOX-GENERATED section below for JWT_SECRET). Full setup:
# docs/email-smtp-runbook.md (Resend is the V1 default; SES documented as
# the alternative). Absent, the stack still starts fine — GoTrue only needs
# a parseable placeholder to boot, not a working one — but no auth email
# (signup confirmation / password reset / email-OTP) actually sends until
# this is set; the script prints a one-line reminder pointing here whenever
# it's absent, every run.
#
# SMTP_PASS is the Resend API key (create one at resend.com after verifying
# your sending domain — see the runbook). Setting it makes
# provision-supabase-stack.sh --apply OVERWRITE the stack's non-functional
# placeholder SMTP_* values with real ones: SMTP_HOST/SMTP_PORT/SMTP_USER
# are hardcoded to Resend's own values in the script (smtp.resend.com / 465
# / resend) since this .env doesn't carry a provider CHOICE, only the key —
# switching to SES means editing the script, not this file, per the
# runbook's Provider B section. SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL/SMTP_SENDER_NAME below are
# optional operator identity (envelope sender + display name); omit either
# to leave the stack's existing value (real or placeholder) untouched.
#
# Provisioning-time only, exactly like COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD above — never
# read by anything except this one script, never enters Coolify's own env
# under this name (the box gets SMTP_PASS as a Coolify-injected secret
# under the SAME name, a separate copy, production_only in
# secrets-manifest.yml). Delete the line after a successful run if you'd
# rather not leave a live Resend key sitting in a local file.
SMTP_PASS=<resend-api-key>
SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL=<noreply@your-domain>
SMTP_SENDER_NAME=<mosko-fintech>

# --- ci-migrate CI trigger (scripts/provision-vps.sh, ADR-072 Option E chunk 2) ---
# CI_MIGRATE_SSH_PUBKEY: the PUBLIC half of a DEDICATED ci_only keypair —
# generate with `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_ci_migrate`
# (no passphrase — this key authenticates a headless GitHub-Actions runner,
# never a human). Distinct from every key in SSH_PUBKEYS above: this one's
# authorized_keys line is a forced command (ADR-072 C1/C3), theirs are not.
# The PRIVATE half is NEVER put here or anywhere in this repo — it goes into
# this repo's GitHub Actions secrets as CI_MIGRATE_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY
# (secrets-manifest.yml `ci_only`; Settings -> Secrets and variables ->
# Actions -> New repository secret), an F/CTO `!`-step done once, by hand,
# outside any script here.
CI_MIGRATE_SSH_PUBKEY=<path-to-id_ed25519_ci_migrate.pub>

# MIGRATOR_SERVICE_UUID / MIGRATOR_TASK_UUID / APP_UUID: non-secret Coolify
# resource identifiers scripts/migrator-orchestrate.sh needs to know which
# Scheduled Task to execute and which application to deploy. These are
# UUIDs Coolify ASSIGNS when you create the migrator Scheduled Task
# (scripts/migrator-scheduled-task.md) and the V1 web app resource
# (docs/deployment-runbook.md §7) — there is no reliable "look it up by
# name" path before those resources exist. Once they DO exist (after the
# stand-up's §6.5 Phase A), `BOX_IP=<box-ip> scripts/record-coolify-uuids.sh
# --apply` looks all four up by resource NAME over the Coolify API and
# writes them here for you — prefer that over reading the dashboard by
# hand. Written verbatim into the box's own /etc/pfin/migrator-trigger.conf
# (group-readable by ci-migrate only) — never Coolify-injected, never a
# container env var.
#
# ⚠ MIGRATOR_SERVICE_UUID is the migrator APPLICATION's own uuid (since
# ADR-072 Amendment 4, 2026-09-18) -- this placeholder used to (wrongly)
# describe it as the Supabase-stack's own uuid, a stale copy from before
# that move. If you need the STACK's uuid (e.g. for scripts/db-shell.sh's
# `--as` path, which execs the `db` service), that is SUPABASE_STACK_UUID,
# below -- a different resource, corrected 2026-09-19 (live finding, §6.8
# step 5).
MIGRATOR_SERVICE_UUID=<migrator-standalone-application-uuid>
SUPABASE_STACK_UUID=<supabase-stack-application-uuid>
MIGRATOR_TASK_UUID=<migrator-db-push-scheduled-task-uuid>
APP_UUID=<v1-web-app-application-uuid>

# ETL_UUID / PDF_RENDER_UUID / PROVIDER_SYNC_UUID: ⚠ NEW, BACKLOG.md §7.36
# item 68 (2026-09-20) -- the three worker Coolify applications
# scripts/provision-worker.sh creates ("pfin-back-etl" / "pfin-pdf-render" /
# "pfin-provider-sync"). Same shape as APP_UUID above: none is guaranteed to
# exist yet. `BOX_IP=<box-ip> scripts/record-coolify-uuids.sh --apply` looks
# all seven UUIDs up by name (not just these three) and writes them here for
# you -- prefer that over reading the dashboard by hand.
ETL_UUID=<pfin-back-etl-application-uuid>
PDF_RENDER_UUID=<pfin-pdf-render-application-uuid>
PROVIDER_SYNC_UUID=<pfin-provider-sync-application-uuid>

# DEPLOY_ON_SUCCESS: Sec-ruled deploy gate (Phase D deploy-gate consult).
# Written into the same box-resident /etc/pfin/migrator-trigger.conf as
# MIGRATOR_SERVICE_UUID / MIGRATOR_TASK_UUID / APP_UUID above -- NOT
# SUPABASE_STACK_UUID, which provision-vps.sh does not consume (that one is
# db-shell.sh's own, see its note above). Default 0 — a successful migration apply withholds the
# app deploy; the first live exercise of ci-migrate's forced command (an
# externally-reachable path holding a [read,write,deploy] Coolify token)
# should do the smallest thing it can, not also fire a real deploy the
# first time it is pulled for real. Flip to 1 at runbook §7 step 7, once
# the deploy leg is deliberately being exercised for the first time.
DEPLOY_ON_SUCCESS=0

# BOX_IP: the production box's IPv4 address. `scripts/provision-vps.sh
# --apply` records this here automatically once the box exists — you should
# not normally need to set it by hand. `scripts/db-shell.sh` (the runbook
# §6 psql-vehicle helper) and `record-coolify-uuids.sh` above both read it
# from here.
BOX_IP=<box-ipv4-address>

# ── PRODUCTION_ONLY SECRETS — scripts/push-production-secrets.sh reads ─────
# THESE, BY NAME, FROM THIS SAME LOCAL .env — this is the "operator's local
# .env is authoritative" secret-injection path §5 of the deployment runbook
# describes. This is NOT a second copy of secrets-manifest.yml's own
# production_only list — it is that list's OWN NAMES, minus the ones
# push-production-secrets.sh itself excludes as owned by a DIFFERENT
# vehicle (its own `EXCLUDED_SUPABASE_STACK` — the Supabase stack's own
# secrets, minted on the box by provision-supabase-stack.sh /
# mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh, never read from here — and its own
# `EXCLUDED_DEFERRED` — `PFIN_DB_PASSWORD`, generated and delivered by
# `scripts/db-role-handoff.sh`, not this script). secrets-manifest.yml
# remains the canonical VALUE-meaning source for each name (what it's for,
# which container(s) receive it, rotation posture) — read it there; this
# file only names which ones need a real value in YOUR local .env before
# running `push-production-secrets.sh --apply`.
#
# `--skip-missing-resource` lets you leave a value blank/absent here until
# the RECEIVING worker resource actually exists (e.g. FMP_API_KEY/
# BLS_API_KEY before `pfin-back-etl` is provisioned) — the script prints
# which names it skipped and why; absence here is not itself an error.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<supabase-service-role-key-placeholder-overwritten-last-by-mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh>
PDF_WORKER_SIGNING_KEY=<openssl-rand-hex-32--SAME-value-pushed-to-both-app-and-pdf-render>
WORKER_ADMISSION_SHARED_SECRET=<openssl-rand-hex-32--SAME-value-pushed-to-both-app-and-provider-sync>
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=<discord-webhook-url--pushed-to-app-etl-and-provider-sync>
FMP_API_KEY=<fmp-api-key--etl-only>
BLS_API_KEY=<bls-api-key--etl-only>
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=<plaid-client-id--provider-sync-only>
PLAID_SECRET=<plaid-secret--provider-sync-only>
SIMPLEFIN_TOKEN=<simplefin-token--provider-sync-only>

# ── BOX-GENERATED — reference only. Do NOT set these here. ─────────────────
# Listed so a stranger who greps this file for "COOLIFY_API_TOKEN" or
# "JWT_SECRET" (both real Coolify-stack values, easy to assume belong in
# every .env-shaped file) finds an explicit answer instead of silently
# adding a line that does nothing. Neither script below reads either name
# from a LOCAL .env at all — setting either here has ZERO effect on any
# provisioning run.
#
# COOLIFY_API_TOKEN — minted ON THE BOX by provision-vps.sh's admin-bootstrap
# step and written to /root/.pfin/coolify.env (mode 600). Every later script
# (provision-supabase-stack.sh, mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh) reads it FROM
# THAT FILE, on the box, on every call — never from here. The one place a
# local COOLIFY_API_TOKEN line ever mattered was the HAND-RUN era of this
# stand-up, before these scripts existed. If your local .env still carries
# one from that period, delete it — it is inert now, not "superseded softly."
#
# JWT_SECRET — minted ON THE BOX by provision-supabase-stack.sh --apply and
# written to /root/.pfin/supabase.env (mode 600; the same file that later
# holds the other Supabase-stack secrets provision-supabase-stack.sh mints).
# mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh reads it FROM THAT FILE to derive the real
# HS256 ANON_KEY/SERVICE_ROLE_KEY JWTs (overwriting the placeholder random-
# hex values provision-supabase-stack.sh sets first). No script has ever
# read a local JWT_SECRET, and none should — this line exists only so the
# name isn't a silent gap for a reader checking "is this var covered
# anywhere."
